---
// /docs/channels — Channels, live human↔agent comms (an additive layer). Pure composition:
// Base → DocsNav(current="channels") → DocSection header → DocDiagram(channels.png) → prose.
// Facts verbatim from the content map — the channel_reply name and the launch flag are EXACT
// (copy, do not paraphrase).
//
// Base-path rule (#1 404 trap): DocDiagram takes a BARE path, prefixed via withBase().
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import DocsNav from "../../components/DocsNav.astro";
import DocSection from "../../components/DocSection.astro";
import DocDiagram from "../../components/DocDiagram.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Channels — Agentry Docs"
  description="Channels add live human↔agent push over the file-watch loop — comments, threaded channel_reply, a permission relay to approve tool-use from the Workbench, and human status changes that steer the agent."
>
  <DocsNav current="channels" />

  <DocSection
    eyebrow="Concept · Channels"
    title="Channels — live human↔agent comms"
    lede="Channels turn the async file-watch loop into a live conversation: you can push a comment, reply in a thread, approve a tool call, or change a task's status and have the agent react — instead of waiting for its next turn."
  >
    <DocDiagram
      src="diagrams/channels.png"
      alt="Diagram of Channels: live push between a human and the agent over the file-watch loop — comments, threaded replies, a permission relay from the Workbench, and human status changes that steer the run."
      caption="Live human↔agent push over the file-watch loop — comments, threaded replies, a permission relay, and status steering."
    />

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Channels are <strong>live human↔agent push over the file-watch loop</strong>. Where the loop on
        its own delivers your edits on the agent's next turn, channels add a live signal. Four kinds:
      </p>
      <ul class="facts">
        <li><strong>Comments</strong> — leave a note on a run's artifact and have it reach the agent.</li>
        <li><strong>Threaded <code>channel_reply</code></strong> — reply in-thread so a back-and-forth stays attached to its context.</li>
        <li><strong>A permission relay</strong> — approve tool-use from the Workbench, instead of only at the terminal.</li>
        <li><strong>Human status changes that steer</strong> — force a task's status and the agent treats it as steering and adjusts what it does next.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Channels are an <strong>additive layer</strong> and a research preview: they need Claude Code
        launched with the flag
      </p>
      <pre class="flag"><code>--dangerously-load-development-channels plugin:agentry@agentry-dev</code></pre>
      <p>
        Without it, everything still works via the async file-watch loop — the files are truth, the agent
        reads your edits on its next turn, and approvals fall back to the terminal.
      </p>
    </div>
  </DocSection>
</Base>

<style>
  .prose {
    max-width: 760px;
  }

  .prose p {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 16.5px;
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin-bottom: 18px;
  }

  .prose strong {
    color: var(--ink);
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .prose em {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .facts {
    list-style: disc;
    padding-left: 22px;
    margin-bottom: 18px;
  }

  .facts li {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.65;
    margin-bottom: 10px;
  }

  .facts strong {
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  /* the launch flag: a mono block so the exact, copy-able string reads as a command */
  .flag {
    background: var(--bg-2);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 10px;
    padding: 14px 16px;
    margin-bottom: 18px;
    overflow-x: auto;
    font-size: 13.5px;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
</style>
